## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about combined constraints and partial knowledge in a policy graph using three-valued logic; requires identifying a concrete satisfying assignment and developing a general propagation strategy for 3-valued logic with conjunction and implication operations.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics for implication (→), conjunction (∧), and disjunction (∨)
- Propagation of unknown (U) values through mixed logical operators
- Fixpoint computation with backtracking to avoid false (F) while preserving unknowns

## Code Example

```javascript
function imp(a,b){ // a->b in Kleene logic
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or  = (x,y) => (x==='T' || y==='T') ? 'T' : (x==='U' || y==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(rules, values){
  let changed;
  do{
    changed = false;
    for(const [name, expr] of rules){
      const newVal = expr(values);
      if(values[name] !== newVal){
        values[name] = newVal;
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  } while(changed);
  return values;
}
```

The algorithm iteratively propagates known values through constraints until a fixpoint is reached, ensuring no constraint evaluates to F while maintaining at least one U value.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to translate graph-based access policy constraints into a three-valued logical solver while managing constraint satisfaction risks. The directed graph structure introduces transitive reasoning requirements, making the problem more realistic than handling independent logical clauses.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic propagation using Kleene semantics (T > U > F)
- Arc-consistency enforcement on directed constraint graphs
- Not-both-true constraint handling and conflict detection
- Backtracking strategies for partial truth assignments

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vals, edges) {
  // vals: {N1:'T'|'F'|'U',...}
  // edges: [[from, to], ...]
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [from, to] of edges) {
      if (vals[from] === 'T' && vals[to] === 'F') {
        return false; // Constraint violated
      }
      if (vals[from] === 'T' && vals[to] === 'U') {
        vals[to] = 'T';
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with non-classical truth in a constrained system, using backtracking under Kleene semantics and additional operators (↔, ⊕) to reveal subtle propagation patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics and derived operators
- Backtracking with constraint propagation
- Handling composite operators like biconditional and XOR under 3-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function imp(a,b){ // a→b = (¬a) ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return (na==='T' || b==='T') ? 'T' : (na==='U' || b==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
}
function orb(a,b){ // max in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b;
}
function land(a,b){ // min in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b;
}
```

